<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-bottom: 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's the Rainy Season</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Where's the Puddle?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Identify the setting and characters in audio storybooks.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify the setting and characters in simple audio stories about rain (e.g., 'The story is about a boy with an umbrella.').
                Can join in simple exchanges about rainy days (e.g., 'What do you wear in the rain?' 'I wear boots.').
                Can answer questions about rainy day items (e.g., 'What do you need?' 'I need an umbrella.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing flashcards or realia of rainy day items (umbrella, boots, raincoat) and pictures of rain and puddles. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and "What is the weather like?" Students respond with simple words or gestures. The teacher says, "It is the rainy season!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words clearly, emphasizing the /r/ sound in 'rain' and 'raincoat' and the /p/ sound in 'puddle'.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a picture of an umbrella and says, "Umbrella. I need an umbrella when it rains." Students repeat chorally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a picture of boots and says, "Boots. I wear boots." Students repeat.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a puddle and says, "Puddle. I like to splash in puddles!" Students repeat.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher introduces 'raincoat', 'wet', 'muddy' similarly.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the vocabulary and context.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we use an umbrella when it's sunny?" (Expected answer: "No.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we splash in a puddle?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's the Rainy Season".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple, short audio storybook (narrative excerpt) about children playing in the rain and finding a puddle. The story should naturally incorporate target vocabulary (puddle, umbrella, raincoat, boots, splash, wet, muddy) and grammar (Present simple: "What do you need?", "I need an umbrella."; Present continuous: "It is raining today.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Narrating current events related to weather: Using the present continuous tense (e.g., 'It is raining. I like puddle-jumping.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces the story: "Today, we are going to listen to a story about a rainy day. Listen carefully to find out who is in the story and where they are."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**First Listening (Gist):** Students listen to the audio story once without any specific task, just to get the general idea.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Gist questions):</b> After the first listen, the teacher asks general questions: "What is the story about?" (Expected: "Rain," "Children," "Puddles.") "Is it sunny or rainy in the story?" (Expected: "Rainy.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Second Listening (Specific Information):** Students listen again. The teacher gives a pre-listening instruction: "Now, listen again. Who are the characters in the story? What is the place where the story happens?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Information matching/True/False):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows pictures of possible characters and settings. After listening, students point to the correct character (e.g., a boy, a girl) and setting (e.g., park, street).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "The story is about a girl and her dog. True or False?" (Students say "False" or point to correct option). "The story happens in a house. True or False?" (Students say "False"). "The story happens outside." (Students say "True").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher explicitly asks: "Who are the characters?" "Where are they?" (Guiding students to say "A boy and a girl," "Outside/In the park/On the street").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present simple (What do you need?), Present continuous (It is raining today.), WH-questions (What do you wear?).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> puddle, umbrella, raincoat, boots, splash, wet, muddy, rain.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Talking about rainy weather: 'I need an umbrella.' Sharing experiences: 'I wear my boots when it rains.'</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening & Drawing/Pointing):</b> The teacher plays short audio clips describing an item or action related to rain. Students listen and either point to the corresponding flashcard/picture on the board or quickly draw a simple sketch.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Audio clip 1: "It is raining! I need this to stay dry. What do I need?" (Sound of rain + 'Umbrella'). Students point to/draw an umbrella.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Audio clip 2: "Look! The children are jumping in the water. They are having fun!" (Sound of splashing + 'Puddle'). Students point to/draw a puddle and children jumping.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Audio clip 3: "My feet are wet, but my boots keep them dry." (Sound of squishing + 'Boots'). Students point to/draw boots.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Listen and Choose):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with two word choices for each sentence. Students listen to the teacher or an audio recording saying a sentence and circle the correct word.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "It is very (rainy / sunny) today." Students circle 'rainy'.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "The children like to (splash / sleep) in the puddles." Students circle 'splash'.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "I wear my (sandals / boots) when it rains." Students circle 'boots'.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen & Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher plays short audio questions (WH-questions) related to rainy days. Each student listens, writes a one-word or short answer, then compares with their partner.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Audio: "What do you need when it rains?" (Students write 'Umbrella' or 'Raincoat'). They check with their partner.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Audio: "What color is the sky when it rains?" (Students write 'Gloomy' or 'Gray'). They check with their partner.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Audio: "What do you wear on your feet in the rain?" (Students write 'Boots'). They check with their partner.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's the Rainy Season".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Listen and Illustrate" – Students listen to a new short, simple audio description of a rainy scene (e.g., "It is raining. A boy named Tom is wearing a yellow raincoat and blue boots. He sees a big, muddy puddle. He wants to splash!") and draw what they hear. The teacher emphasizes listening for details about the character, their clothes, and the setting. This task directly connects to the theme "Where's the Puddle?" and scenario "It's the Rainy Season."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities & Teacher Actions:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains the activity: "Listen to my description of a rainy day. Draw what you hear on your paper. Who is there? What are they wearing? What do they see?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the audio description two times.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students draw the scene and characters based on what they heard.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After drawing, students can show their drawing to a partner and say one or two words about it (e.g., "Puddle," "Boy," "Raincoat"). This encourages listening and identification through non-verbal and minimal verbal responses.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Listen and Illustrate" activity and a brief "Listen and Answer" mini-quiz.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher plays a final, very short audio clip (1-2 sentences) describing a different simple rainy scene. Students are given a paper with 2-3 simple WH-questions (What, Who, Where) and check or circle the correct picture/word from options. (e.g., Audio: "It is rainy. A girl has a red umbrella. She is standing next to a big puddle." Questions: "Who has an umbrella?" (picture of girl/boy) "What is next to the girl?" (picture of puddle/flower)).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies main characters and setting from the audio description (Specific Objective).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Understands key vocabulary related to rain (umbrella, puddle, boots, rain).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Answers simple WH-questions accurately based on audio input.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket by circling a smiley face (😊) or a neutral face (😐) next to two statements:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can understand who is in a story about rain."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can understand where the story happens."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about rain did I hear and learn today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult about listening to the stories today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? (e.g., use of visuals, audio clarity, engagement with story).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face with listening comprehension or vocabulary? (e.g., speed of audio, distinguishing sounds, understanding WH-questions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to support listening development? (e.g., slowing down audio, providing more pre-listening vocabulary, using more TPR).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Great job listening today, everyone! Next time, we will look at a story about rainy days in a book to practice reading the words we heard today and learn more about where the puddle is!" (This sets the stage for Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension, using the same vocabulary and theme.)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>